\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{quadratic-forms}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Right translation and conjugate double cosets}

\p{Let #{G} be a group, let #{\Gamma,K\leq G}, and fix #{a\in G}.
Write #{[x]_K} for the \vocabk{double coset}{fcap-004P}
#{\Gamma xK}. Right translation induces a bijection

##{\begin{aligned}
T_a:\Gamma\backslash G/K&\longrightarrow
\Gamma\backslash G/(a^{-1}Ka),\\
[x]_K&\longmapsto[xa]_{a^{-1}Ka}.
\end{aligned}}

Its inverse sends #{[z]_{a^{-1}Ka}} to #{[za^{-1}]_K}.
The left subgroup stays #{\Gamma}; neither subgroup is assumed
normal, and #{G} need not be commutative.}

\subtree{
\taxon{Proof}
\p{The map #{c_a(k)=a^{-1}ka} is a group automorphism:
#{c_a(1)=1} and

##{(a^{-1}ka)(a^{-1}\ell a)=a^{-1}(k\ell)a,}

while its inverse is #{h\mapsto aha^{-1}}. Thus
#{a^{-1}Ka=\{a^{-1}ka:k\in K\}} is a subgroup: it contains
#{1}, and the displayed product and
#{(a^{-1}ka)^{-1}=a^{-1}k^{-1}a} keep products and inverses
inside it.}

\p{Suppose #{y=\gamma xk} with #{\gamma\in\Gamma} and
#{k\in K}. Associativity and cancellation give

##{ya=\gamma(xa)(a^{-1}ka).}

Since #{a^{-1}ka\in a^{-1}Ka}, this proves that #{T_a}
is independent of the representative. For the proposed inverse,
suppose #{w=\eta z h}, where #{\eta\in\Gamma} and
#{h\in a^{-1}Ka}. Choose #{k\in K} with #{h=a^{-1}ka}.
Then the inverse's witnesses are #{\eta} and #{k}, since

##{wa^{-1}=\eta z(a^{-1}ka)a^{-1}
=\eta(za^{-1})k.}

The inverse assignment is therefore well defined. On a source
representative its composite with #{T_a} gives
#{(xa)a^{-1}=x}; on a target representative the other composite
gives #{(za^{-1})a=z}. Every quotient class has a representative,
so both composites are the identity.}
}

\p{For the \vocabk{finite adelic class sets}{fcap-004P}, fix a
nondegenerate rational quadratic form and a
\vocabk{compatible compact-open reference tuple}{fcap-004C} #{U}.
For each #{H\in\{O,SO,\Spin\}}, use the same ambient group
#{H_f(U)} of \ref{fcap-004F}, its own
\vocabk{rational diagonal subgroup}{fcap-004K} #{\Gamma_H}, and
its actual product subgroup #{K_H}. For #{a_H\in H_f(U)}, the
bijection is

##{\Gamma_H\backslash H_f(U)/K_H
\longrightarrow
\Gamma_H\backslash H_f(U)/(a_H^{-1}K_Ha_H).}

It changes the right subgroup inside this fixed group, without
replacing the chosen O, SO or Spin rational image. It is a
bijection of sets; no quotient topology, approximation or
class-number assertion is involved.}

\p{The restricted groups and rational diagonals have adelic context in
\citet{Example 2.3, p. 3, and Theorem 3.6, pp. 6-8}{conrad2011adelic}.
The conjugation calculation and inverse above are direct group
arguments, not statements attributed to that passage. TauCeti's
\leanref/tauceti{DoubleCoset.quotientConjRight}
is the general equivalence with this orientation. The three
adelic specializations are
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicOrthogonalDoubleCosetConj},
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpecialOrthogonalDoubleCosetConj}
and
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.QuadraticMap.OrthogonalCompactOpens.finiteAdelicSpinDoubleCosetConj}.
In their targets, the inverse conjugation automorphism sends
#{k} to #{a_H^{-1}ka_H}, as in the displayed proof.}
